          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Yes, on one point. The guidance I could retrieve disagrees about which reliever to use in mild asthma. It does not disagree about whether reliever-only treatment is appropriate. On that question the retrieved guidance is consistent.\n\n**Where the guidance agrees.** The asthma guidance summary (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*) says that treating asthma with a short-acting bronchodilator alone is no longer appropriate at any severity. It states that every adult and adolescent with asthma should receive inhaled-corticosteroid-containing therapy. The reasons given are that reliever-only treatment leaves the underlying inflammation untreated and is linked to severe attacks, even in people with infrequent symptoms. Needing several reliever canisters a year is described as a risk marker in its own right.\n\n**Where it differs.** The same document reports a real disagreement about the preferred reliever in mild asthma:\n- Global strategy guidance names an inhaled corticosteroid combined with a fast-acting long-acting bronchodilator as the preferred reliever across steps.\n- Some national panel guidance says a short-acting reliever alongside a regular controller remains appropriate in mild disease.\n\nNote that the second position still includes a regular controller. It is not reliever-only treatment.\n\n**Limits of what I found:**\n- **Individual bodies:** The retrieved summary names only the \"global strategy\" and \"national panel\" sources. It does not name each society, so I cannot tell you which specific organisations hold each position.\n- **Children:** I found no passage on reliever-only treatment in children, so I cannot say what guidance recommends for them.\n- **Evidence strength:** The retrieved passages do not grade the evidence behind either position, so I cannot say how strong the support is for each. For that, you would need the original guideline documents, which list their evidence grades.\n\nIf an asthma reliever stops working, or an attack returns within a few hours, the condition summary says to seek prompt medical assessment. Call emergency services straight away if someone has trouble speaking in full sentences, is breathless at rest, is drowsy or confused, or has blue lips or fingers.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
